  "worked_solution": "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8}{x+1}={x}^{2}-2x+8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8=(x+1)({x}^{2}-2x+8).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-2x+8$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={2}^{2}-4(1)(8)=-28<0.\n$$\n\nSo the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-2x+8$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8=(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2x+8).\n$$",
